Let $A$ be a solid abelian group and let $P$ be a compact projective object in $\mathbf{Ab}(A)$. We need to show that $P$ is not flat with respect to the solid tensor product.

First, note that since $P$ is compact, it is also coherent, so it has a finite filtration $0=P_0\subseteq P_1\subseteq\cdots\subseteq P_n=P$ such that each $P_i/P_{i-1}$ is a cyclic group.

Now, suppose for the sake of contradiction that $P$ is flat with respect to the solid tensor product. Then for any object $Q$ in $\mathbf{Ab}(A)$ and any morphism $f:P\to Q$ in $\mathbf{Ab}(A)$, there exists a morphism $g:Q\to P$ such that $f\circ g=id_Q$.

Since $P$ is compact, we can assume that $g$ is a compact morphism, i.e., $g$ factors through a finite direct sum of copies of $P$. Moreover, since $P$ is projective, we have $P\cong\Hom_A(A,P)$, so we can identify $P$ with the set of homomorphisms from $A$ to $P$.

Now, consider the following diagram:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
P \arrow[r, "f"] \arrow[d, "g"'] & Q \arrow[d, "h"] \\
P \arrow[r, "id_P"'] & P
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

where $h$ is the identity morphism on $Q$. Since $f$ is a morphism in $\mathbf{Ab}(A)$, we have $f\circ g=id_P$, so we can fill in the diagram as follows:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
P \arrow[r, "f"] \arrow[d, "g"'] & Q \arrow[d, "h"] \\
P \arrow[r, "id_P"'] & P
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

Since $P$ is compact, we have $g(1_P)=1_Q$, so we can cancel $g$ and $h$ to get:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
P \arrow[r, "f"] \arrow[d, "id_P"'] & Q \arrow[d, "id_Q"] \\
P \arrow[r, "id_P"'] & P
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

This is a contradiction, since we have $f\circ id_P=f\circ g=id_Q=h\circ id_Q$, but $id_P$ and $h$ are not equal in $\mathbf{Ab}(A)$. Therefore, we have shown that $P$ is not flat with respect to the solid tensor product.

This completes the proof that the compact projective objects in the category of solid abelian groups are not flat with respect to the solid tensor product.

Sure! Here's a step-by-step guide to create a simple calculator in Angular:

1. Create a new Angular project:

```
ng new calculator-app
```

2. Change to the new project directory:

```
cd calculator-app
```

3. Generate a new component for the calculator:

```
ng generate component calculator
```

4. Open the `calculator.component.html` file and add the HTML for the calculator. Here's an example:

```html
<div class="calculator">
  <input type="text" [(ngModel)]="displayValue" />
  <div class="buttons">
    <button (click)="onClick('7')">7</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('8')">8</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('9')">9</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('/')">/</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('4')">4</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('5')">5</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('6')">6</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('*')">*</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('1')">1</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('2')">2</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('3')">3</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('-')">-</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('0')">0</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('.')">.</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('+')">+</button>
    <button (click)="onClick('=')">=</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

5. Open the `calculator.component.ts` file and add the TypeScript code for the calculator. Here's an example:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-calculator',
  templateUrl: './calculator.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./calculator.component.css']
})
export class CalculatorComponent {
  displayValue = '0';
  operand1: number | null = null;
  operator: string | null = null;

  onClick(value: string) {
    if (value === '+' || value === '-' || value === '*' || value === '/') {
      this.operator = value;
      this.operand1 = parseFloat(this.displayValue);
      this.displayValue = '0';
    } else if (value === '.' && this.displayValue.indexOf('.') === -1) {
      this.displayValue += '.';
    } else if (value === '=' && this.operand1 !== null && this.operator !== null) {
      const operand2 = parseFloat(this.displayValue);
      switch (this.operator) {
        case '+':
          this.displayValue = (this.operand1 + operand2).toString();
          break;
        case '-':
          this.displayValue = (this.operand1 - operand2).toString();
          break;
        case '*':
          this.displayValue = (this.operand1 * operand2).toString();
          break;
        case '/':
          this.displayValue = (this.operand1 / operand2).toString();
          break;
      }
      this.operand1 = null;
      this.operator = null;
    } else {
      if (this.displayValue === '0') {
        this.displayValue = '';
      }
      this.displayValue += value;
    }
  }
}
```

6. Add some CSS styles to the `calculator.component.css` file to make the calculator look nice. Here's an example:

```css
