Sure! Here's an example of how you can create an Angular directive to focus an element based on a value passed by an input binding of type number:
```
import { Directive, Input, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appFocus]'
})
export class FocusDirective {
  @Input('appFocus') focusValue: number;

  constructor(private elementRef: ElementRef) {}

  ngAfterViewInit() {
    const element = this.elementRef.nativeElement;
    const focusIndex = this.focusValue - 1;

    if (focusIndex >= 0 && focusIndex < element.children.length) {
      element.children[focusIndex].focus();
    }
  }
}
```
In this directive, we're using the `@Input` decorator to bind a value of type number to the `focusValue` property. This value will be passed in through the directive's input binding.

In the `ngAfterViewInit` lifecycle hook, we're retrieving the native element using the `ElementRef` and getting the children of the element using the `children` property. We're then using the `focusIndex` variable, which is calculated by subtracting 1 from the `focusValue`, to find the child element that should be focused.

Finally, we're calling the `focus()` method on the child element to set focus on it.

To use this directive in your template, you can add the `appFocus` directive to an element and bind a value to the `focus` input binding, like this:
```
<div appFocus [focus]="1">Focus me!</div>
```
This will set focus on the second child element (index 1) of the element that has the `appFocus` directive applied.

